50560-E.5 Simplified Mnemonics for Branch Instructions
50561-Mnemonics are provided so that branch conditional instructions can be coded with the condition as part of the instruction mnemonic rather than as a numeric operand. Some of these are shown as examples with the branch instructions.
50562-The mnemonics discussed in this section are variations of the branch conditional instructions.
50563-E.5.1 BO and BI Fields
50564-The 5-bit BO field in branch conditional instructions encodes the following operations. � Decrement count register (CTR) � Test CTR equal to zero � Test CTR not equal to zero � Test condition true � Test condition false � Branch prediction (taken, fall through)
50565-The 5-bit BI field in branch conditional instructions specifies which of the 32 bits in the CR represents the condition to test.
50566-To provide a simplified mnemonic for every possible combination of BO and BI fields would require 210 = 1024 mnemonics and most of these would be only marginally useful. The abbreviated set found in Section E.5.2 Basic Branch Mnemonics, is intended to cover the most useful cases. Unusual cases can be coded using a basic branch conditional mnemonic (bc, bclr, bcctr) with the condition to be tested specified as a numeric operand.
--
52692-Memory access ordering. The specific order in which the processor performs load and store memory accesses and the order in which those accesses complete.
52693-
52694-Memory-mapped accesses. Accesses whose addresses use the page or block address translation mechanisms provided by the MMU and that occur externally with the bus protocol defined for memory.
52695-
52696-Memory coherency. An aspect of caching in which it is ensured that an accurate view of memory is provided to all devices that share system memory.
52697-
52698-Memory consistency. Refers to agreement of levels of memory with respect to a single processor and system memory (for example, on-chip cache, secondary cache, and system memory).
52699-
52700:Memory management unit (MMU). The functional unit that is capable of translating an effective (logical) address to a physical address, providing protection mechanisms, and defining caching methods.
52701-
52702-Microarchitecture. The hardware details of a microprocessor's design. Such details are not defined by the PowerPC Architecture.
52703-
52704-Mnemonic. The abbreviated name of an instruction used for coding.
52705-
52706-Modified state. When a cache block is in the modified state, it has been modified by the processor since it was copied from memory. See MESI.
52707-
52708-Munging. A modification performed on an effective address that allows it to appear to the processor that individual aligned scalars are stored as little-endian values, when in fact it is stored in big-endian order, but at different byte addresses within double words. Note that munging affects only the effective address and not the byte order. Note also that this term is not used by the PowerPC Architecture.

